-- title:
--   Integrality over ℂ[j] of q-expansion quotients of modular forms
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of finite index containing the translation matrix $T = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$, let $k$ be an integer, and let $g, h$ be modular forms of weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, with $h \ne 0$. Assume that for every point $\tau$ of the upper half plane $\mathbb{H}$ the function $z \mapsto \lVert g(z)/h(z)\rVert$ is bounded above along the filter of punctured neighbourhoods of $\tau$ (i.e. $g/h$ is locally bounded near each point of $\mathbb{H}$ after removing the point itself). Write $\tilde g, \tilde h \in \mathbb{C}[[q]]$ for the $q$-expansions of $g$ and $h$ of period $1$ at $i\infty$, regarded as Laurent series in $\mathbb{C}((q))$. Then the quotient $\tilde g/\tilde h \in \mathbb{C}((q))$ is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated by the single element [`ModularCurve.jqModC ℂ`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15), namely $q^{-1}$ times the image in $\mathbb{C}[[q]]$ of the integral power series $E_4^3 \cdot (\text{the inverse unit power series attached to the Dedekind }\eta\text{-product})$, i.e. the $q$-expansion $q^{-1} + 744 + 196884\,q + \cdots$ of the modular invariant $j$.
--
--   This is the integrality statement underlying the classical fact that a modular function for a finite-index subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ which is holomorphic on $\mathbb{H}$, with poles only at the cusps, is integral over $\mathbb{C}[j]$; here it is formulated at the level of $q$-expansions in $\mathbb{C}((q))$. It is used in the construction of the complex points of modular curves, via [`ModularCurve.ComplexPlaceDictionaryOf.exists_pt_eq_of_mem`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ComplexPlaceDictionaryOf.exists_pt_eq_of_mem). The proof cites the identification of [`ModularCurve.jqModC ℂ`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) with $\tilde{E_4}^3/\tilde\Delta$ and the integrality of $\tilde g/\tilde\Delta^m$ over $\mathbb{C}[\tilde{E_4}^3/\tilde\Delta]$ for forms of weight $12m$ on arithmetic subgroups.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_isIntegral_adjoin_jqModC_qExpansion_div_of_forall_isBoundedUnder_of_finiteIndex.lean

